Synchronization proof: each thread j writes exactly `values[j]` before reaching the unconditional barrier, and all 96 threads reach it. The barrier makes those shared-memory writes visible to thread zero before it reads indices 0 through `valid - 1`; every such entry was initialized by its corresponding thread from an in-bounds input element. Threads outside that range write zero without reading input, and all shared indices stay within 0–95.

When `valid == 0`, all threads still initialize their slots and execute the barrier. Thread zero then executes an empty summation loop and writes positive zero to the output. The same-stream copy, kernel, and result copy execute in that order, and the host retains the pinned buffers until the completion event finishes.

Threads 70–95 skip the entire guarded region in the original kernel: they perform no input load, shared-memory store, barrier, or output write there. Threads 0–69 encounter a divergent `__syncthreads()`, violating the block-wide synchronization contract; this is undefined behavior, not a guarantee that every device hangs. Successful immediate launch checks detect submission errors, not correct barrier participation or successful completion.

In the replacement, thread i initializes `values[i]` before every thread reaches the unconditional barrier. The barrier makes those writes visible to thread zero, which reads only indices 0 through valid−1. For valid == 0, thread zero reads no shared entries and explicitly writes 0.0f to the output; the kernel still launches with exactly 96 threads.

